## What changed, and why it supersedes the existing plan draft

Full detail and code citations are in the review doc's new section — summary here:

- **Phase 1 (generation) — call every analyzer, tag status, never decide.** Every registered
  analyzer is called unconditionally (sat included, no special-casing). Every call produces one
  ballot entry; **order does not matter** (retracts positional binding). Each entry carries an
  explicit status (enablement + error condition). The engine may validate ("no `(a)` anywhere?",
  "no live+enabled `(b)` anywhere?") but makes no decisions from that.
- **Phase 2 (the binding/anchor getter, successor to `saturationEntry`) — interpret, decide,
  build.** Check statuses → find the binding analyzer → generate the anchor's `VariantCapacities`
  **merged per variant, matched by `VariantName`** (`(a)` fields from sat's entry for that name,
  `(b)` fields from the binding analyzer's entry for that same name; model-level PRC/RC/SC always
  from the binding analyzer) → remove disabled/non-live results (done here, not at Phase-1
  packaging — safer). **No analyzer-level sort needed at this stage** — confirmed nothing sorts the
  ballot slice itself, only per-variant lists, which already key off `PerReplicaCapacity`/`Cost`
  and read correctly once the anchor is built.
- **No stored `.Anchor` field at all** — this is the single biggest structural difference from both
  existing designs (the committed code and the Aug 4 fold-in). The anchor is derived on demand,
  which is also why `withSatEntry` likely doesn't need the 87-site fix the Aug 4 fold-in added —
  needs confirming once implementation starts, not asserted as certain.

## Specific asks for the plan doc

1. Commit 1 (`engine_v2.go`): replace with the uniform Phase-1 loop above — no anchor-building code
   in the engine at all, no `effectiveEnabled`-gated ballot-entry exclusion at generation time.
2. Commit 2 (binding function): replace `saturationEntry`'s successor with the Phase-2 getter above,
   per-variant merge, positional-binding language removed.
3. QM: option (b) — make it an error to enable QM under the new code. Test blast radius checked —
   no test found that exercises `optimizeQueueingModel` end-to-end, so no test carnage expected; an
   e2e-test sweep is still owed (see Open items). Note: the Aug 4 fold-in already designed a
   `refuseQueueingModel` dispatch-refusal using the existing `emitSafetyNetMetrics` idiom — check
   whether that design already satisfies this ask or needs adjusting for the new mechanism.
4. Liveness/failure policy: "no live analyzers → do nothing" as the base behavior; do not kill WVA
   for this (confirmed no existing code does today); reuse `emitSafetyNetMetrics`
   (`engine.go:1828+`, "Strategy 1: use previous desired-replicas value if available") as the
   primitive, rather than inventing something new.
5. `AnalyzerName` validation gap (unconstrained field, silent fallback to deprecated V1 on any
   typo) — flag as a candidate follow-up issue; separate, small, standalone PR; not part of this
   plan.
6. Partial scale-from-zero under `[TA]`-alone: document the finding that the existing
   `scalefromzero` engine already, incidentally, covers this (per-VA `isInactive` check, no
   model-level pre-filter) — recommend relying on it as the reactive safety net. Recommend the
   TA-side proactive complement (persist `lastPerReplicaSupply` *and* `Cost`/`AcceleratorName` on
   `variantState`, same eviction window as `lastFittedB`) as the proper proactive fix — note the
   corrected version (an earlier draft of this idea had a ranking bug: defaulting only `PRC` while
   leaving `Cost` at zero makes `costEfficiency = 0/PRC = 0`, ranking the variant *cheapest*, not
   safely last — both fields need the fallback together).
7. rescale.go: add the per-variant-merge correctness requirement as an explicit test case — a
   `[TA]`-only ballot with rescale enabled, asserting `AcceleratorName` resolves via the anchor even
   though TA's own result leaves it empty.

## Open items still needing resolution (flagged, not resolved by me)

- e2e-test sweep for the QM "make it an error" decision (unit-test sweep done, e2e not yet).
- Targeted check on whether a "do nothing" cycle actually leaves
  `Status.DesiredOptimizedAlloc.NumReplicas` untouched at its last-good value.
- Flapping-risk check: could the normal engine's own decision, on the cycle right after
  `scalefromzero`'s direct actuation, see stale/estimate-based state and scale back down? Not
  traced.
- A **newly-found, pre-existing bug in sat-v2 itself**, independent of this refactor: sat's own
  `aggregateByVariant` rebuilds its `variantCost`/`variantAccel` maps fresh every cycle from that
  cycle's `ReplicaMetrics` only — so sat's P0-store/compatible-sibling branches (which do estimate
  `PerReplicaCapacity` for a zero-replica variant) never backfill `Cost`/`AcceleratorName` for that
  same variant. Worth a standalone fix, not part of this plan — flagging so it doesn't get lost.

